Inter process communication 
---------------------------

* Basically process are of 2 types

	1. Independent process
	
	2. Co-operating process 

	Independent process means the process which is not affected by other process execution. 

	Co-operating process is a process which is effected by other process execution.

	In general one process can not accesses the other processes data, OS willn't allow one process to access other process information.
 	
	But if there is a need of one process should access the other process data then there is a requirement of proper channel. 

	The mechanisms available for communications are 

		* pipes
		* named pipe(FIFO)
		* message queue
		* shared memory			


		* semaphores (it is for process synchronization) 

		pipes and named pipes are using file management.... 

		shared memory are based on memory management....If OS is not supporting memory management it is difficult to use shm....

		message que is not depending on file management, memory management......................................................

		


PIPES 
-----

* Operations involved in pipes are 

	1. create the pipe
	
	2. Write the data
	
	3. Read the data

	
All these IPC mechanisms are created in Kernel space...

Note : using PIPE only related process (parent and child) can communicate non related process can't.	

	related process means like parent and child process...

	

How to create pipes

	by using pipe() call...


	in pipe it is unidirectional dataflow...

	data flows from write end to read end...

	
	
	pipe()
	------

	* Reading from empty pipe (if any process reading data from empty pipe) process get blocked. 
	
	* writing into full pipe (if pipe is completely full with data) process blocked. 





* Pipe created means temporary buffer created in kernel space ...
  
  Only with the help of file descriptor we can pipe ...


Observations
-----------

1. Close the write end of the pipe after that try to read the data from the pipe.
	
	close(fd[1]);

	then read returns zero.

2. Close the read end of the pipe after that try to write the data into pipe (In this case use while(1))

	close(fd[0])

	normally it terminates the process by recieving SIGPIPE signal.

	signal(SIGPIPE, isr);

	void isr (int n) {

		
	}

	normally isr terminates the program.




Limitations of Pipe
-------------------

* Using pipe only related process can communicate non related process cannot communicate.

* When pipe is created, there is no name existing in the file system, it is just a temporary buffer created in kernel space.





































	
